<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chrysocheir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 872 or 878</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chrysocheir was the last leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a>, a nephew and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karbeas">Karbeas</a>, whom he succeeded in 863. Allied with the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of the frontier, he led deep raids into Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> around 869, and rejected the peace terms offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bathys-ryax">Battle of Bathys Ryax</a>, after which Paulician power collapsed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name, also written Chrysocheres, Chrysocheris or Chrysocheiros, means goldhand. According to Peter the Sicilian he was the nephew and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karbeas">Karbeas</a>, which indicates that he had married his first cousin, a practice the Byzantine Church strongly condemned. After the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> launched in 843 by the Empress-regent Theodora, Karbeas and many of his followers had fled to the Muslim border <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> and set up an independent principality centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a>, from which he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> on the side of the Muslims. Nothing is known of Chrysocheir's life before he succeeded his uncle. Like Karbeas, he may have served in the Byzantine army in his youth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Chrysocheir raided deep into Byzantine territory, as far as the western coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and the neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, where he is reported to have stabled his horses in the Church of Saint John the Evangelist. When Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> the Macedonian sent envoys to offer peace in 869/70, Chrysocheir is said to have demanded that the emperor first give up the eastern half of his lands in Asia Minor. Peter the Sicilian reports spending nine months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> at about this time to arrange the release of high-ranking prisoners; some scholars think he led the embassy, others do not.</p><p>In 871 Basil himself attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> but failed to take it. In 872/3 the Domestic of the Schools Christopher won a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulicians">Paulicians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bathys-ryax">Battle of Bathys Ryax</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chrysocheir was killed in the battle by a common soldier called Poullades. His head was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where Basil reportedly shot three arrows into it with his bow. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Tephrike</a> itself fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> only six years later, ending the Paulician principality, and some modern scholars place Chrysocheir's defeat and death in the same year as the city's fall. His memory is commonly thought to survive in the Byzantine epic Digenes Akritas as Chrysoberges, the Muslim paternal grandfather of its hero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chrysocheir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Karbeas died in 863 his nephew <b>Chrysocheir</b> led even bolder raids, reaching Nicaea and Ephesus around 869, where his men stabled horses in the church of Saint John.</blockquote><a href="/state/paulician-state">Paulician state</a></li></ul></section>
